    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>An asset register is only worth something if it matches the kit on the floor. The device moved between comms rooms. The laptop that left with a leaver. The switch swapped and never logged. Each is a line in your register that lies. So ask which tool records the count while you are still standing at the rack.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>An IT asset audit matches what is there to what the register or CMDB says.</li><li>The valuable result is the gap list: ghost assets and unrecorded devices.</li><li>Serials and tags recorded on the spot beat figures typed up later.</li><li>Decommission and disposal need their own proof, including WEEE carrier details.</li><li>ISO 27001 Annex A treats asset management and secure disposal as controls. So the audit is security proof, not just a stock-take.</li><li>Quickler records the count and writes the report. A competent person decides what is compliant.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The point",
      "heading": "What an IT asset audit is for",
      "html": "<p>An IT asset audit confirms the hardware you think you own exists, sits where the record says, and carries the serial and tag on the register. It supports licensing, insurance values, refresh plans and information security. When kit drifts out of the record, you lose track of what needs patching, what holds data and what has left the building.</p><p>Software does not make the register right. You do. Software makes the count easier to record, harder to skip, and faster to match to the CMDB.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Reconciliation",
      "heading": "The gap list is the point",
      "html": "<p>A count that matches the register perfectly is rare and a little suspect. The value is in the gaps. A ghost asset is a record with no device, often kit thrown out without updating the register. An unrecorded device is real kit with no line in the register. That is worse, because it may hold data nobody tracks.</p><p>Quickler records each device as you find it, with the serial and tag photographed on the spot. The office can watch the count live. Matching to the CMDB is your decision. Quickler gives you timed proof to make it with.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "ISO 27001",
      "heading": "Asset management is a security control",
      "html": "<p>Under ISO 27001, asset management and secure disposal are Annex A controls. A certification auditor expects an asset inventory, assigned owners, and proof that end-of-life media were disposed of securely. A photographed, timed record of what exists and what was retired is that proof.</p><p>Quickler records the audit with a time, the auditor, the place and a photo for each device. It does not certify you or decide if a control is met. Your competent person and your certification body do. Check the current standard, as Annex A references have changed.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Decommission and WEEE",
      "heading": "End-of-life needs its own proof",
      "html": "<p>Retiring a device is more than deleting a register line. Under the Waste Electrical and Electronic Equipment Regulations, IT hardware is WEEE. It must go by an authorised route, with the carrier and disposal recorded. If a device held data, record the secure wipe or destruction, and keep a certificate.</p><p>Quickler can record the retirement in the same Check: the wipe or destruction method, the WEEE carrier and a photo of the collection or certificate. It does not wipe anything or issue a certificate. It does not replace your disposal provider's paperwork. Check the current WEEE Regulations and your data protection duties.</p>"
    },
